## Constants and Basic Input/Output

### Constants

Use `const` to mark a variable as read-only:

```c
const double TAX_RATE = 0.08;
```

The preprocessor `#define` creates a text substitution that the compiler replaces before compilation:

```c
#define MAX_STUDENTS 100
#define GREETING "Hello"
```

### Reading input with scanf

`scanf` reads formatted input from standard input. The address-of operator `&` is required for non-pointer variables:

```c
int age;
printf("Enter your age: ");
scanf("%d", &age);
```

`scanf` stops at whitespace. To read a whole line use `fgets`:

```c
char name[100];
fgets(name, sizeof(name), stdin);
```

### A complete example

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a, b;
    printf("Enter two numbers: ");
    scanf("%d %d", &a, &b);
    printf("Sum: %d\n", a + b);
    return 0;
}
```

### Return value of scanf

`scanf` returns the number of fields successfully read — useful for loops:

```c
while (scanf("%d", &n) == 1) {
    // process n
}
```

