## Conditionals: if / else if / else

C makes decisions with `if` statements. The condition is any expression that evaluates to a nonzero (true) or zero (false) value:

```c
int score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
    printf("A\n");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    printf("B\n");
} else {
    printf("Keep practicing\n");
}
```

### Relational and logical operators

| Operator | Meaning          |
|----------|------------------|
| `==`     | Equal to         |
| `!=`     | Not equal        |
| `&&`     | Logical AND      |
| `||`     | Logical OR       |
| `!`      | Logical NOT      |

```c
if (x > 0 && x < 10) { ... }
if (year % 4 == 0 && (year % 100 != 0 || year % 400 == 0)) { ... }
```

### Common pitfall: `=` vs `==`

A single `=` assigns; it is an assignment expression that always yields the assigned value:

```c
if (x = 5)     // WRONG — always true, assigns 5 to x
if (x == 5)    // RIGHT — comparison
```

### Dangling else

An `else` binds to the **nearest** unmatched `if`. Braces remove all ambiguity:

```c
if (a) {
    if (b) printf("a and b");
} else {
    printf("not a");
}
```

### Ternary operator

A compact one-line conditional:

```c
int max = (a > b) ? a : b;
```

---

**Next up:** loops — repeating work with `while` and `for`.

## Loops: while, for, do-while

### while

```c
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
    i++;
}
```

### for

The `for` loop bundles initialisation, condition, and update:

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
}
```

### do-while

`do-while` runs the body at least once before checking the condition:

```c
int n;
do {
    scanf("%d", &n);
} while (n != 0);
```

### Choosing a loop

- `for` when you know the iteration count.
- `while` when the number of iterations depends on a condition checked first.
- `do-while` when the body must run at least once.

### Loop with scanf

A very common C pattern reads until end of input:

```c
int n;
while (scanf("%d", &n) == 1) {
    printf("got %d\n", n);
}
```

### Infinite loops and exits

```c
while (1) {          // deliberate infinite loop
    ...
    if (done) break;
}
```

`break` exits the loop; `continue` skips to the next iteration.

---

**Next up:** loop patterns like sentinels and accumulation.

## Loop Patterns: Accumulation, Sentinel & Counting

### Accumulation

Sums and products accumulate a running result inside a loop:

```c
int total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    total += i;          // running sum
}
```

### Counting

Count occurrences that match a condition:

```c
int evens = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (values[i] % 2 == 0) evens++;
}
```

### Sentinel loop

A **sentinel** is a special value that signals the end of input:

```c
int n, sum = 0, count = 0;
while (scanf("%d", &n) == 1) {
    if (n == -1) break;   // -1 is the sentinel
    sum += n;
    count++;
}
```

### Min/max tracking

```c
int n, min, max;
scanf("%d", &n);
min = max = n;
while (scanf("%d", &n) == 1) {
    if (n < min) min = n;
    if (n > max) max = n;
}
```

### Flag pattern

```c
int found = 0;             // 0 = false
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (values[i] == target) { found = 1; break; }
}
if (found) printf("found\n");
```

---

**Next up:** nested loops and break/continue.

## Nested Loops and break / continue

### Nested loops

A loop inside another loop runs the inner loop fully for each outer iteration:

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        printf("(%d,%d) ", i, j);
    }
    printf("\n");
}
```

### break

`break` exits the **innermost** loop only:

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 3) break;     // stops at 3
    printf("%d ", i);      // prints 0 1 2
}
```

### continue

`continue` skips the rest of the current iteration and moves to the next:

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 2) continue;  // skips 2
    printf("%d ", i);      // prints 0 1 3 4
}
```

### Nested break — labelled exits

`break` only breaks one level. To escape nested loops, use a flag:

```c
int done = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 5 && !done; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 5; j++) {
        if (arr[i][j] == target) { done = 1; break; }
    }
}
```

### Pattern printing

Nested loops shine at drawing shapes:

```c
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) printf("*");
    printf("\n");
}
```

