## Functions: Declaration, Definition & Return

Functions group reusable logic. Every C program needs a `main`; every other function exists to help it.

### Anatomy

```c
// return_type name(parameters) { body }
int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}
```

### Declaration vs definition

A **declaration** (prototype) tells the compiler a function exists. A **definition** provides its body.

```c
int add(int a, int b);   // declaration (prototype)

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", add(2, 3));  // works thanks to the prototype
    return 0;
}

int add(int a, int b) {          // definition
    return a + b;
}
```

### Parameters are copies

C passes arguments **by value** — the function works on copies:

```c
void change(int x) { x = 100; }

int main(void) {
    int n = 5;
    change(n);
    printf("%d\n", n);   // still 5
    return 0;
}
```

### void functions

A function that returns nothing uses `void`:

```c
void greet(const char *name) {
    printf("Hello, %s\n", name);
}
```

### Early return

```c
int abs_val(int x) {
    if (x < 0) return -x;
    return x;
}
```

---

**Next up:** pointers — the feature that lets functions modify their callers' data.

## Return Values and Passing Data

### A single return value

C functions return exactly one value (or `void`). To return more, pass pointers to output parameters.

### Passing by pointer (call by reference simulation)

```c
void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

int main(void) {
    int x = 1, y = 2;
    swap(&x, &y);      // pass addresses
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);   // 2 1
    return 0;
}
```

### const parameters

`const` promises the function will not modify the data:

```c
int string_length(const char *s) {
    int len = 0;
    while (s[len] != '\0') len++;
    return len;
}
```

### Arrays as parameters

An array parameter decays to a pointer to its first element:

```c
int sum_array(int arr[], int n) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += arr[i];
    return total;
}
```

### Returning pointers

You may return a pointer to memory that outlives the function — static variables, passed-in buffers, or `malloc`'d memory. Never return a pointer to a local variable:

```c
int *bad(void) { int x = 5; return &x; }   // WRONG: dangling pointer
```

