## Structs: Grouping Related Data

A `struct` bundles related values into one type.

### Definition

```c
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};
```

### Using structs

```c
struct Point p1;
p1.x = 3;
p1.y = 4;

struct Point p2 = {10, 20};       // initialise in order
struct Point p3 = {.y = 5};       // designated initialiser
```

### Copying structs

Structs can be copied with `=`:

```c
struct Point p4 = p1;             // copies x and y
```

### Structs and functions

```c
struct Point add_points(struct Point a, struct Point b) {
    struct Point result = {a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y};
    return result;
}
```

### typedef

`typedef` gives a struct a shorter name:

```c
typedef struct {
    char name[50];
    int age;
} Person;

Person alice = {"Alice", 30};
```

### Arrays of structs

```c
Person class[3] = {
    {"Ada", 36}, {"Grace", 38}, {"Alan", 41}
};
printf("%s\n", class[0].name);
```

### Pointers to structs

Use `->` to access members through a pointer:

```c
Person *p = &class[1];
printf("%s\n", p->name);    // equivalent to (*p).name
```

---

**Next up:** array/string/struct exercises.

## Multi-dimensional Arrays & String Functions

### Matrices

```c
#define ROWS 2
#define COLS 3

int matrix[ROWS][COLS] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};
for (int r = 0; r < ROWS; r++) {
    for (int c = 0; c < COLS; c++) {
        printf("%d ", matrix[r][c]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}
```

### Passing 2-D arrays to functions

You must specify all dimensions except the first:

```c
void print_matrix(int m[][COLS], int rows) { ... }
```

### Array of strings

```c
char *weekdays[] = {"Mon", "Tue", "Wed"};
printf("%s\n", weekdays[1]);   // Tue
```

### String comparison

```c
if (strcmp(s1, s2) == 0) {
    printf("equal\n");
}
```

### Copying safely

```c
char dest[10];
strncpy(dest, "hello", sizeof(dest) - 1);
dest[sizeof(dest) - 1] = '\0';   // ensure termination
```

### Searching

```c
char *pos = strchr("hello", 'l');   // points to first 'l'
```

### Reversing a string in place

```c
void reverse(char s[]) {
    int len = strlen(s);
    for (int i = 0; i < len / 2; i++) {
        char t = s[i];
        s[i] = s[len - 1 - i];
        s[len - 1 - i] = t;
    }
}
```

