## File Input and Output

File I/O uses a `FILE *` handle returned by `fopen`.

### Opening and closing

```c
#include <stdio.h>

FILE *fp = fopen("data.txt", "r");   // r = read, w = write, a = append
if (fp == NULL) {
    perror("fopen");
    return 1;
}
// ... work ...
fclose(fp);
```

### Reading

```c
char line[256];
while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), fp) != NULL) {
    printf("%s", line);
}
```

`fgets` reads a whole line including the newline, or until the buffer is full.

### Writing

```c
FILE *out = fopen("out.txt", "w");
fprintf(out, "value: %d\n", 42);
fclose(out);
```

### Reading numbers

```c
int n;
while (fscanf(fp, "%d", &n) == 1) {
    // process n
}
```

### Modes

| Mode | Meaning                    |
|------|----------------------------|
| `r`  | Read (file must exist)     |
| `w`  | Write (truncates)          |
| `a`  | Append (create if missing) |
| `r+` | Read and write             |
| `rb`/`wb` | Binary modes         |

### Check for errors

```c
if (fclose(fp) == EOF) {
    fprintf(stderr, "error closing file\n");
}
```

---

**Next up:** the preprocessor and macros.

## Header Files and Program Organisation

Real C programs are split into multiple files: headers for declarations, source files for definitions.

### Project layout

```
project/
├── main.c
├── utils.c
└── utils.h
```

### Header file (utils.h)

```c
#pragma once

int add(int a, int b);
int multiply(int a, int b);
```

### Source file (utils.c)

```c
#include "utils.h"

int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

int multiply(int a, int b) {
    return a * b;
}
```

### Main file (main.c)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "utils.h"

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", add(2, 3));
    return 0;
}
```

### Compiling multiple files

```bash
gcc main.c utils.c -o app
```

### Why separate them

- `main.c` sees only the declarations it needs.
- Compiling is incremental — one change recompiles one file.
- Interfaces (`.h`) stay clean while implementations (`.c`) can change.

### Linking errors

If a function is declared but never defined, the **linker** fails with an "undefined reference" error. Check that every declared function has a definition in some `.c` file.

